    },
    {
      question: 'How do you handle OFFICIAL:Sensitive and PROTECTED data classifications?',
      answer: "Australia's Protective Security Policy Framework (PSPF) defines the handling requirements for OFFICIAL, OFFICIAL: Sensitive and PROTECTED data. We build OFFICIAL and OFFICIAL: Sensitive workloads in Australian cloud regions with appropriate encryption, access controls and audit logging. PROTECTED systems need infrastructure and services assessed through ASD's IRAP program and personnel with the relevant security clearances; we have neither, so for PROTECTED work we can only contribute components that need no clearance under a cleared prime contractor. Either way, we design with data classification in mind from the start, including labelling, handling rules for API consumers and the access control architecture needed to enforce classification boundaries.",
    },
    {
      question: 'Can you work within agile delivery frameworks required by DTA and state digital strategies?',
      answer: "Yes. The DTA's Digital Service Standard (and equivalent state frameworks like the NSW Digital Design System and the Victorian Digital Service Standard) require an iterative delivery approach centred on users. We're comfortable operating in the delivery model these frameworks prescribe: discovery phases producing validated problem statements and user research insights; alpha phases producing rough prototypes tested with real users; beta phases delivering progressively complete services; and live phases with ongoing continuous improvement. We use Jira and Confluence for delivery transparency (standard across most government ICT environments), and we design around the reporting cadences and governance artefacts that government programme management offices ask for.",
    },
